AUDIO: 'future updates for free, and a private Discord community full of artists working towards the same goal as you. And actually, we just passed 33,000 students, which is honestly crazy to me'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at this point. So, to celebrate that milestone, the whole program is 33% off right now until the end of the month. So, click the link down in the video description before it\'s gone. I don\'t want you to miss out on this. All right,'

[06:09] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A new reference image of a woman stretching, with a blank canvas and the timer at 1:11.
AUDIO: 'with my shameless plug over, let\'s move on to the next pose. And this one\'s about the third mistake, which is drawing timidly. Watch my strokes here. They\'re pretty long, right? They\'re'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'confident and they go all the way through the body. What most people do instead is little, short, careful [screaming] strokes, chicken scratch lines, using 20 times more lines than you would actually need to draw the'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole figure. And the result, of course, is what we\'re all familiar with, like this fuzzy mess with no structure. And the reason, of course, is fear. Chicken scratch lines are basically the result of being scared of drawing a wrong line. But in a 2-minute study, a wrong line'

[06:47] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A partial sketch of the stretching woman, showing the main body outline, with the timer at 0:33.
AUDIO: 'will cost you nothing, really. You\'re not making art here, you\'re training. So, I know it\'s easier said than done, but commit to the line. Draw it fast and confident, all the way through, and if it comes out bad, just undo it, you'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'know, control Z and try it again. Or just draw it over if you\'re on pen and paper. The goal is to capture the pose with as few lines as possible. That\'s the exercise. This way, each line carries [music] more weight and the'

[07:12] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: The sketch is nearly complete, with red arrows pointing to the feet, emphasizing their importance.
AUDIO: 'fewer you use, the faster you get. And also, straight lines are faster and more accurate than curves, typically. So, whenever the silhouette lets you get away with one, basically, use it. You\'ll notice a lot of my forms ends up'

[07:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slightly angular for that reason. And that\'s by design. And maybe you\'re wondering about this app that I\'ve been using here. It\'s something that I built with a developer over the last couple of months, and you can actually grab it'

[07:37] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A new reference image of a woman sitting on a box, with a blank canvas and the timer at 1:33.
AUDIO: 'from my store for a few bucks now. And our school students get special treatment, so I sent you a coupon for half off the whole price. I\'ll put the link to this below the Art School link in the video description. You should check it out, but not before you watch'

[07:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the final pose. And here it [music] is. And with this one, I want to show you what all of this is actually building towards because this is the part that maybe is not so obvious at the beginning. So, your quick reference'

[08:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drawing is not the artwork [music] unlike gesture drawing. What we\'re working on here is the first step of every figure that you will ever draw. They\'re allowed to be ugly, right? It\'s like the sketch, basically. They\'re'

